    <section id="cs-top">
        <h1>Mod Heat</h1>
        <h2>A brand-aware trend product I took from an early 2024 concept through executive approval and a Q4 release.</h2>
        <figure class="story-illustration case-header-visual">
            <%= image_tag "mod-heat-focal-point.jpg", alt: "Hand-drawn hand reaching toward a focused signal held in a shared bowl" %>
        </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-problem">
        <h3>The tension</h3>
        <p>At agencies, a trend response often starts with one person noticing something in the news and bringing it to a meeting. The team then weighs brand fit, timing, and risk. That takes time, and the moment can pass while the discussion is still going.</p>
        <p>The agency already had listening tools. Mod Heat's job was to support the judgment that followed and connect it to a possible action.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-role">
        <h3>What I owned</h3>
        <p>I came up with Mod Heat in February 2024 and pitched it to the executive team that spring. I defined the product and scoring model, then directed the work through its Q4 release. I also connected it to our emerging agent work, which later influenced how we thought about Nexus.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-insight">
        <h3>The insight</h3>
        <p>I started with the way brand teams already talk. The same news story can be useful for one client, irrelevant to another, and uncomfortable for a third. Audience, positioning, risk tolerance, and timing all change the answer.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-system">
        <h3>The agent-assisted turn</h3>
        <p>Mod Heat scans news, curated industry sources, and social conversation every day and clusters what it finds into recognizable trends. It then scores each trend against a specific brand with a framework we called F.I.T.S.: familiarity, impact, timing, and safety. The output is a ranked view of moments by relevance, risk, and potential reach.</p>
        <figure class="mod-heat-visual">
            <%= image_tag "mod-heat-case-study.gif", alt: "Mod Heat interface showing brand-aware trend signals" %>
        </figure>
        <p>A promising signal could flow straight into a Brand Agent workflow. The agent, which carries the client's voice, standards, audience nuances, and risk guardrails, could summarize the moment, weigh it against the brand context, and draft activation concepts and first tactics, while the team made the final call.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-changed">
        <h3>The outcome</h3>
        <p>The first release shipped internally in Q4 2024, and Mod Op <a href="https://www.modop.com/press/mod-op-expands-ai-suite-with-launch-of-mod-heat-ai-coach-and-ecommerce-agent/" target="_blank">launched Mod Heat publicly</a> in October 2025 as part of its AI suite. It is now <a href="https://www.modop.com/orion-ai/mod-heat/" target="_blank">part of Orion</a>. The product shortened the path from signal to action. Brand teams had a standing view of the moments worth discussing for each client, and an important one could move straight into an agent-assisted workflow. Client-facing moments are also served through Nexus MCP, the platform's Model Context Protocol integration, so a client's ranked moments can reach other AI tools and agents rather than living only in the Mod Heat interface. The context and decision logic we built here also gave our team an early model for applying brand context inside an AI product, which became useful as Nexus took shape.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cs-learned">
        <figure class="story-illustration">
            <%= image_tag "mod-heat-signal-field.jpg", alt: "Hand-drawn streams of cultural signals narrowing toward one clear point" %>
        </figure>
        <h3>What I learned</h3>
        <p>Executive approval came in April, and the first release shipped in Q4. Most of my work between those points was deciding what belonged in the first version, what could wait, and how to keep the original idea intact as the scope changed.</p>
        <p>It was also the first product we built with Cursor. We picked it up in mid-2024, and I built in a framework I had never used, learning it as the product took shape.</p>
    </section>

